1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
During the first half of 1958, Convair Fort Worth conducted a series of studies at the

2
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Oak Ridge National Laboratories to gain additional radiation shielding information.

3
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
This was the final phase of the airborne shielding program performed with the aircraft shield

4
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
test reactor as a radiation source.

5
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The program began in September 1955 when Convair's nuclear test aircraft first carried the aircraft

6
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
shield test reactor along.

7
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
The nuclear test aircraft was a conventionally powered B-36, modified to carry a shielded

8
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,000
crew compartment for the five-man crew, a half-scale crew compartment, and the aircraft

9
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
shield test reactor and its nuclear instrumentation.

10
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The crew compartment utilized lead and rubber as shielding materials.

11
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,000
The cylindrical half-scale crew compartment was carried to simplify dose-rate analysis.

12
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Sleeves of different wall thickness were slipped into the half-scale crew compartment to obtain

13
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
dose rates as a function of wall thickness.

14
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
The radiation source, the ASTR, was a water-cooled, water-moderated reactor designed to operate

15
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,000
horizontally. Shield tanks which surrounded the reactor core could be

16
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:06,000
filled or drained in flight to change the source pattern. An external lead

17
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:14,000
shield augmented the gamma shielding. The ASTR was rated at 1 megawatt maximum

18
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:23,000
thermal power. Thus assembled, the NTA was used to study the effects of air

19
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
structure, and ground scattering of gamma and neutron radiation. A three-phase

20
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
program was carried out to separate the individual scattering components and

21
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
determine their contribution to the total dose rate. The first studies

22
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
involved the ASTR and the half-scale crew compartment to learn the air and

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
ground scattering effects. The second included the NTA to determine the air,

24
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:58,000
ground and structure effects. The flight program was intended to determine the

25
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
air and structure effects. At first it was thought that by a simple process of

26
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
subtraction, the three individual components of scattering could be

27
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:17,000
separated into air, structure and ground effects.

28
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:24,000
But it was soon learned that the structure was changing the air and ground scattering

29
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:32,000
to such an extent that it was impossible to separate the three effects directly.

30
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Additional information on air effects alone was necessary.

31
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Convair then planned to take the ASTR and the crew compartments to the tower shielding

32
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
facility at Oak Ridge, Tennessee.

33
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:53,000
With the ground and structure effects removed, the air scattering component could be determined.

34
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Auxiliary equipment to support the ASTR tower experiment was designed and fabricated.

35
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
It consisted of a truss to carry the two crew compartments aloft, a reactor support system

36
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:13,000
designed to rotate the ASTR through 360 degrees.

37
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,000
And a heat exchanger for reactor cooling.

38
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
As fabrication and testing of equipment neared completion, the crew compartment was removed

39
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
from the NTA and made ready for the move to Oak Ridge.

40
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,000
The ASTR normally was housed in a loading pit, from which it was remotely installed

41
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:43,000
in the NTA. Following a final checkout of the modified control system the 35,000

42
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:47,000
pound reactor was removed from the loading pit and ready for its

43
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:54,000
cross-country trip. A truck convoy was assembled to carry all the equipment

44
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
from Fort Worth to the Oak Ridge site.

45
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:23,000
The convoy arrived at the tower shielding facility 44 hours after leaving Convair.

46
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Preparations for the first phase of the experiment began immediately.

47
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,000
This phase consisted of source term mapping near the ground to compare with results obtained

48
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,000
at Fort Worth.

49
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Radiation detection instruments were placed in constant temperature control cans and mounted

50
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
at various positions to determine the fast neutron and gamma dose rates and thermal neutron

51
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
flux.

52
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Prior to reactor startup, the heat exchanger and moderator water systems were thoroughly

53
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:07,000
checked. Shield tanks were filled to obtain the correct configuration.

54
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:37,000
When the system reached the desired altitude of 186 feet, the mapping phase of the experiment was ready to begin.

55
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:48,000
An intercom system warned personnel to leave the outside area and go to the shielded control room.

56
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Standard procedure at the tower shielding facility calls for close surveillance of local weather conditions with radar.

57
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:04,000
When the radar observer was satisfied that the local weather was clear, the reactor engineer

58
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
started up the reactor.

59
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:16,000
Shem and dynamic rods were adjusted until desired power level was reached.

60
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,000
The reactor was rotated to various positions to obtain a map of the radiation patterns

61
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
for different reactor configurations.

62
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:31,000
These values were used as source terms in the analysis of the experiment and for correlations

63
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,000
with the data from the NTA program.

64
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Cables carried the radiation signals more than 575 feet to the recording instruments.

65
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:48,000
These instruments, located in the control room, recorded the data from the fast neutron,

66
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
gamma, and thermal neutron detectors.

67
00:07:52,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Personnel remained in the control room until the reactor was shut down and lowered to the ground.

68
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:07,000
Phase two of the tower experiment involved the three major components of the NTA.

69
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:16,000
The ASTR, the crew compartment, and the half-scale crew compartment in their same relative position as in the aircraft.

70
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
Radiation detectors were located in the same positions as in the NTA program.

71
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:31,000
The tower studies simulated the flight portion of the NTA program with only the airplane structure missing.

72
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Under these simulated conditions, it was possible to isolate the air-scattered radiation component.

73
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
Comparing information obtained at the tower with the ground and flight experiments,

74
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:51,000
it was possible to determine the structure and ground effects and their interrelationships.

75
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:58,000
The information obtained during the airborne shielding program has been useful in two important ways.

76
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:05,000
It has been directly applied to shield design problems and has verified design methods.

77
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:12,000
This program has contributed greatly toward developing efficient shield systems

78
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:18,000
and constitutes a major step forward in development of nuclear-powered aircraft.
